  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1102 PM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nThe winter storm forecast for tonight and tomorrow is on track with \nlittle change. Light snow is falling on the Park Range with an inch \naccumulation so far, and light snow is falling on I-70 over Vail \nPass. The Winter Storm Warning is still in effect for the Park and \nElkhead Mountains through 5 AM Sunday for 10 to 15 inches new snow \nwith locally higher amounts possible. Likewise, the Winter Weather \nAdvisories for the Flat Tops, and Gore and Elk Mountains including \nthe I-70 mountain corridor through Vail and Vail Pass also remains \nin effect for the same period for four to eight inches new snow with \nlocally higher amounts possible. This system has plenty of moisture \navailable from the atmospheric river (AR) impacting Oregon, and the \nshortwave dropping down from the Pacific Northwest in the morning \nhas strong upper-level dynamics from the left exit region of the jet \nmoving overhead. Look for the heaviest snowfall over the mountains \nbetween about 8 AM and 2 PM and we could see a periods with snow \nsqualls as the shortwave passes. Snow squalls can produce heavy snow \nand strong winds leading to near zero visibilities in the snow and \nblowing snow along with roads flash freezing, quickly becoming \ntreacherous. If you are on the road and receive a warning for a snow \nsquall, best practices are to find a location where you can safely \npull off and wait out the storm. They typically pass quickly in 10 \nto 20 minutes, and then you can continue your travel as road \nconditions permit. \n\nThe 00Z models are just coming in now and are indicating the jet \nstreak dipping farther south that the previous model runs have \nsuggested. Will need to watch this overnight as it could push the \nsnow band farther south and west bringing impacts to the Tavaputs, \nGrand Mesa and the Uncompahgre Plateau. Stay tuned for updates on \nthis as there could be some big changes. \n\nSUNDAY AND BEYOND:\n\nThe transitory ridge moves in from the west Sunday morning to push \nthe showers on east out of eastern Utah and Western Colorado. but a \nfew orographic showers may continue through the day along the divide \nfrom lingering moisture. A weak system passes to the north Monday \nbringing only a slight, less than 25% chance of showers to the \nnorthern mountains with little if any accumulation. The next system \nto impact the region comes in Tuesday night into Thursday. It has a \ntap on moisture from an AR moving in through the LA Basin and will \nhave the most significant impacts on the southern face of the San \nJuan Mountains. It's too early to say significant this system will \nbe as the models very significantly from run to run. The latest run \nhas tracked the low farther north over the Four Corners which would \nbring a much more impactful storm, but confidence is low at this \npoint. Stay tuned for updates on this system through the weekend. By \nMonday we should have a much better handle on this one.",
